NODE SIDE

  MCLAUTO LOG 63 udp responder on 192.168.4.1:47100
  MCLAUTO LOG 22661 udp frame class=3 major=1 payload=10 from 192.168.4.2
  MCLAUTO LOG 22665 udp frame class=3 major=1 payload=10 from 192.168.4.2
  MCLAUTO LOG 22669 udp frame class=3 major=0 payload=10 from 192.168.4.2
  MCLAUTO LOG 22673 udp frame class=3 major=0 payload=10 from 192.168.4.2
  MCLAUTO LOG 22677 udp frame class=3 major=0 payload=10 from 192.168.4.2
  MCLAUTO LOG 22681 udp 4 bytes refused rc=8
  MCLAUTO LOG 26709 udp 32 bytes refused rc=2
  MCLAUTO LOG 28712 udp 18 bytes refused rc=3
  MCLAUTO LOG END held=13 dropped=0

  MCLAUTO RESULT state=RUNNING phase=QUIESCE zero_prior=true recovered=5
                 log_dropped=0
  MCLAUTO VALUE own_source_ref=1804657767 provenance=LOCAL
  MCLAUTO VALUE peer_ip=33859776 provenance=FROM_BEARER

  The three refusals carry DIFFERENT reason codes -- 8, 2 and 3 -- rather than
  one generic rejection. A responder that answered every malformed datagram
  identically would be indistinguishable from one that was not parsing.

WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES

  Stable IP-DATAGRAM carriage on the autonomous board, over a real radio,
  against an implementation independent of the reference code, INCLUDING Link
  major 1 -- which until 2026-09-08 no independent implementation had ever
  decoded, because link-v0.md's layout section still said "link_major is 0 for
  this draft" and the clean-room implementation was written from that sentence.

  The run is zero-prior: the node was told nothing about the peer and learned
  its address from the datagram that arrived (provenance FROM_BEARER). The
  node's own port is harness configuration and is recorded as such.

WHAT IT DOES NOT ESTABLISH

  Stranger discovery over IP. MCL defines no global discovery port and this rig
  does not invent one. Nor E6: the Python was written by the same author, from
  the same documents, as the C.
